AB  - The integration of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI), particularly Large Language Models (LLMs) and Diffusion Models, into Mobile Edge Computing (MEC) signifies a transformative shift from “Connected Intelligence” to “Generative Intelligence” in the 6 G era. Despite its potential to revolutionize user experiences and network orchestration, the deployment of GenAI at the edge is hindered by a stark dichotomy between the massive computational demands of foundation models and the inherent resource constraints of mobile devices. This paper provides a state-of-the-art survey of the GenAIMEC ecosystem, proposing a holistic four-layer taxonomy: OnDevice GenAI, Infrastructure Edge Deployment, Cloud-EdgeEnd Collaboration, and GenAI-driven Network Management. We systematically analyze key enabling technologies, including model compression (quantization and pruning), split learning, and semantic communications. Furthermore, we investigate the dual role of GenAI in security and identify critical unresolved challenges such as semantic reliability and the “Green Edge” sustainability dilemma. This survey provides a comprehensive roadmap for researchers to navigate the complexities of deploying generative intelligence in future wireless landscapes.
