AB  - Monthly self-reported work logs are a standard instrument in Chinese knowledge-intensive firms, used both as attendance evidence and as inputs to measuring individual contribution. Their textual nature, however, makes them susceptible to “Impression Management” (IM), in which employees may use rhetorical complexity to inflate perceived workload. We refer to this specific phenomenon as Semantic Inflation: the case where a log’s textual volume and complexity are disproportionate to its recognized output. Manual auditing of such logs is labor-intensive and subjective, whereas routing sensitive internal logs to cloud-based Large Language Models (LLMs) is often restricted by enterprise compliance policies. To explore an alternative, we present a privacy-preserving, on-premise inference workflow that uses quantized open-weight LLMs (e.g., Qwen-2.5-7B) on a single consumer-grade GPU (no model training or fine-tuning is performed). We define the Semantic Inflation Rate (SIR), a decision-support indicator that combines a Semantic Deviation Index with log length and leader-approved work hours, to flag logs whose textual elaboration is disproportionate to their recognized output. Using a longitudinal dataset of 8,603 monthly-report entries from a single R&D division of one Chinese technology enterprise (2014–2024), we observe an Inverse Precision Effect: within the Qwen-2.5-7B family, both the 2-bit and 4-bit variants align more closely with leader-approved hours than the 8-bit variant (full-sample Pearson  $R \approx 0.50$ , 0.50, and 0.41 for the 2-, 4-, and 8-bit variants, respectively). A test for differences between dependent correlations indicates that the 2- and 4-bit variants are each significantly better aligned than the 8-bit variant but are statistically indistinguishable from each other, so we summarize the ordering conservatively as Q $2~\approx $  Q4 > Q8. These correlations are moderate. We read them as consistent with information-bottleneck reasoning, namely that aggressive post-training quantization may implicitly attenuate low-density rhetorical content, though the mechanism itself is not demonstrated here. SIR is positioned as a decision-support tool that helps managers triage logs for human review. Practical implications: the workflow is feasible on commodity hardware within an air-gapped network, lower-bit quantization does not necessarily degrade alignment on this task, and SIR can surface candidate outliers for human-in-the-loop review while preserving data sovereignty.
ER  - 

