AB  - Edge deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) is bounded by tight limits on per-device memory, compute throughput, energy draw, and inter-node bandwidth that no single device can satisfy alone. We organize the optimization landscape under five technical pillars covering model compression, model partitioning, collaborative inference, hardware-aware optimization and scheduling, and adaptive inference. Following a screening procedure modeled on PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses), a corpus of 52 peer-reviewed studies spanning 2018–2026 together with 20 production inference systems is assessed, with explicit attention to PagedAttention, continuous batching, and GGUF quantization. A cross-paper coverage analysis indicates that latency and memory targets dominate the literature, whereas only 6 of the 52 reviewed papers tackle fault tolerance and only 5 treat privacy. From these observations we extract six open gaps and an agenda that addresses fault-tolerant execution, privacy-preserving distribution, Key-Value (KV) cache–aware serving, and carbon-aware scheduling.
ER  - 

