AB  - The advancement of clinical decision support systems depends on the essential integration of medical images together with their corresponding textual reports. The paradigm of Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (MRAG) shows promise because it uses external knowledge sources to support Large Language Model (LLM) responses. Recent advances such as MMed-RAG demonstrate up to 43.8% improvement in factual accuracy for medical vision-language tasks. This research investigates three main approaches to implement MRAG for medical image understanding through (1) API-based access to strong general-purpose Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) (e.g., GPT-4V, Gemini), (2) local deployment of Vision Language Models (VLMs) with parameter-efficient fine-tuning (e.g., LoRA, DoRA), and (3) a hybrid method that uses pre-trained embedding models (e.g., CLIP, MedCLIP, BiomedCLIP) for local structured knowledge base retrieval alongside a local VLM for secure context-aware response generation. The evaluation assesses these methods through their documented capabilities and literature-based model efficacy and data privacy aspects for Protected Health Information (PHI) and evaluates their deployment complexity and cost alongside scalability and customizability and latency and transparency and knowledge updatability. Through a conceptual case study and decision framework, we analyze the trade-offs for typical healthcare institutions. The research establishes particular medical use cases which demonstrate the best fit for each approach under strict Institutional Review Board (IRB) regulations and data privacy standards including HIPAA and GDPR. This research establishes a decision framework for healthcare ecosystem MRAG solution selection and implementation to address specific healthcare challenges.
ER  - 

