AB  - Transformer models are now the most common way to model sequences in natural language processing, vision, and multimodal learning. In recent years, their use has quickly moved to long-context situations, where the length of the input has grown from a few thousand tokens to hundreds of thousands or even millions of tokens. This change has completely changed how efficient Transformers are. Early research focused mostly on making self-attention less computationally complex. However, recent large-scale deployments have shown that memory usage during inference, especially due to key-value (KV) caching, is now the main problem for scalability, latency, and energy efficiency. This survey looks at modern Transformer systems from a costand frugality-focused point of view, with a focus on changes that will happen between 2023 and 2025. We first look again at the memory and computational costs of Transformer models during training and inference, focusing on the change from attention computation to memory-bound inference. Next, we look at some recent improvements in efficient attention mechanisms, such as hardware-aware exact attention (like FlashAttention-3) and latent or compressed attention formulations (like Multi-head Latent Attention in DeepSeek-V3) that cut down on KV cache memory by a lot. We also look at new architectural paradigms like state space models (Mamba, Mamba-2) and hybrid attention-SSM architectures, which make it possible to model sequences in linear time and get rid of or reduce KV caching.Lastly, we talk about low-cost training and inference methods, such as low-precision computation, 1-bit large language models, and system-level KV cache optimizations for serving large amounts of data. This survey gives a current look at the design principles that will shape the next generation of efficient and frugal sequence models by bringing together model-level, architectural, and system-level points of view.
