AB  - Small Language Models (SLMs) have surfaced as effective and efficient substitutes for Large Language Models, particularly in low-power, memory-limited, and privacyconscious settings. This paper offers a thorough examination of the architectures, optimization strategies, and deployment methodologies that facilitate high-performance on-device and edge-based inference utilizing SLMs. Contemporary encoder- and decoder-based SLMs-including DistilBERT, TinyBERT, MobileBERT, MiniLM, Qwen2, and Llama-3 8B-are analyzed with an emphasis on their architectural design decisions, compression techniques, and computational trade-offs. Essential optimization approaches such as pruning, quantization, and knowledge distillation are investigated, along with deployment-specific improvements including operator fusion, hardware-aware tuning, and adaptive model scaling. Practical deployment workflows for edge devices like the Raspberry Pi 5 are also outlined to illustrate real-world applicability. Additionally, the paper underscores significant areas where SLMs are transforming edge intelligence, including on-device voice assistants, customer support systems, predictive text engines, industrial IoT analytics, and healthcare diagnostics. The findings indicate that SLMs provide low-latency, privacy-preserving, and energy-efficient AI functionalities, underscoring their importance in the forthcoming generation of intelligent edge systems.
