AB  - Enterprise database applications are typically mediated by Object–Relational Mapping (ORM) frameworks such as MyBatis rather than by raw SQL, yet most natural-language interfaces to data target single SQL strings and bypass the mapper layer. This paper formulates the pioneering task of Text-to-Mapper, generating executable MyBatis mapper files from natural-language requirements given database schemas and Java entity specifications. To support this setting, a high-quality dataset, MyBatis-Spider together with an evaluation pipeline that exercises candidates through a parse-build-execute-align runtime chain. To capture execution-level semantics, an execution-based metric, Object Semantic Alignment (OSA), compares the Java objects produced by generated and reference mappers. Our empirical study of direct-transfer baselines highlights recurring field-column-type binding errors; motivated by this, we introduce Across-Struct-Table (AST), a compact two-dimensional representation that makes these correspondences explicit. Systematic experiments with commercial and open-weights large language models show that AST yields consistent OSA improvements by reducing field-level mismatches. With parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) on top of AST, mid-scale open-weights models narrow the gap to larger general-purpose models, and in some settings match or slightly exceed them on this benchmark, suggesting a viable path toward controllable, on-premise Text-to-Mapper systems.
