AB  - Existing vision-based traffic accident analysis methods suffer from limited generalization to long-tail accident types and lack semantic-level explanations for their outputs. This paper proposes CrashExpert, an Expert-Rationale Supervised Fine-Tuning framework built upon multimodal large language models. Given a single surveillance image, CrashExpert pairs visual tokens extracted by Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct with structured expert rationales organized into a six-level reasoning chain (Perception, Category, Pattern, Severity, Cause, Conclusion). A hybrid fine-tuning strategy-full fine-tuning of the projection layer combined with LoRA adaptation of the LLM-enables the model to simultaneously output accident type labels and generate interpretable structured rationales. Experiments on our privately constructed dataset Traffic-Crash-Sight demonstrate that CrashExpert achieves the highest recall on the safetycritical pedestrian-vehicle category (80.49 %) among all compared methods, while uniquely providing readable reasoning traces for post-hoc accident review.
