AB  - Parameter Efficient Fine Tuning offers a computationally viable foundation for Large Language Models to deliver adaptive cognitive scaffolding and real-time feedback in Problem-Based Learning environments. Educational institutions frequently face computational resource constraints and model hallucination risks when attempting to deploy generative models for personalized instruction. This study presents a systematic literature review examining how parameter-efficient model adaptation techniques, particularly Low-Rank Adaptation, support adaptive gamification and cognitive scaffolding within Problem-Based Learning. Following Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses guidelines, a comprehensive search across Scopus, IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, SpringerLink, and Google Scholar identified 1,147 records, from which 45 peer-reviewed journal articles published between 2022 and 2025 were selected after applying AMSTAR quality criteria. The synthesized findings reveal that Low-Rank Adaptation tuned models like Llama-3.2 and Mistral-7B outperform traditional baselines in classroom dialogue analysis while reducing hardware overhead. Across the reviewed studies, PEFT-enabled adaptive gamification increased student motivation by up to 35.3 percent, enhanced knowledge retention by 32%, automated 61.7% of instructional content creation, and achieved 93.1% accuracy in mitigating hallucination risks via multi-agent scaffolding. System designers and educators can leverage these open-source adaptation frameworks to implement reliable, resource-efficient adaptive gamification in localized learning management systems.
