AB  - Deploying large Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) and Large Language Models (LLMs) on resource-constrained hardware remains challenging due to their high computational, memory, and energy requirements. Conventional compression techniques, including pruning, quantization, and architectural optimization, can reduce these costs effectively; however, they often rely on manually designed heuristics and rule-based strategies. Such approaches face difficulties in efficiently exploring the large search spaces of modern neural architectures, frequently leading to sub-optimal trade-offs between model accuracy and efficiency. To address these limitations, Reinforcement Learning (RL) has emerged as an effective framework for automated model compression by formulating the optimization process as a Markov Decision Process (MDP). Although numerous RL-based compression methods have recently been proposed, existing survey studies primarily categorize the literature according to the compression type, such as pruning or quantization, while providing limited analysis of the underlying RL mechanisms and design strategies. To address this gap, this survey presents, to the best of current knowledge, the first comprehensive RL-centric taxonomy for model compression. The state-of-the-art methods are systematically analyzed across four key dimensions: (1) MDP modeling strategies, including sequential layer-wise, topology-aware, multi-agent, dynamic runtime, and efficient environment modeling formulations; (2) action space design, covering quantization-oriented, pruning-oriented, joint compression, dynamic input-adaptive, and hierarchical action spaces; (3) reward function engineering, including accuracy-oriented, compression-aware, hardware-aware, multi-objective, and task-specific reward formulations; and (4) reinforcement learning algorithms, encompassing value-based methods, actor–critic approaches, policy-gradient techniques, Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL), and hybrid/model-based RL frameworks. The survey further examines recent developments ranging from pruning and mixed-precision quantization in Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to token pruning, context compression, and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) pruning in Vision Transformers (ViTs) and LLMs, highlighting the relationships between RL design choices and compression performance. The review also describes the literature-selection scope, summarizes representative quantitative results, and discusses key limitations affecting scalability, transferability, benchmarking, and deployment. Overall, this paper provides a structured RL-centric perspective on automated model compression, identifies current challenges and research gaps, and outlines promising directions for developing scalable, adaptive, and hardware-aware compression systems.
ER  - 

