AB  - The ability to summarise longitudinal psychiatric clinical notes will be vital for timely and informed clinical decisions during multiple hospital admissions, as they will carry important information related to symptom trajectory, treatment response and risk evolution. The unstructured and temporally disjointed nature of these records, however, poses significant problems for manual review and for current automated summarization systems that often handle the process of summarizing individual admissions separately, and potentially produce temporally inconsistent or unsupported information. In this work, a longitudinal clinical summarization framework is presented which integrates the heuristic-based instruction tuning with a Hybrid Retrieper-Augmented Generation (Hybrid RAG) model to enhance factual grounding and temporal consistency. Admission-scoped patient history is built from raw psychiatric notes, and weakly supervised data pairs are created with clinically motivated heuristics, which allows using parameter-efficient finetuning methods such as QLoRA to train a LLM on this data. The multi-stage retrieval pipeline with sparse retrieval, dense semantic retrieval, and cross-encoder re-ranking are used to retrieve the evidence to support the generation of the summary during the inference stage, to guide the generation of the admission-specific summary. The longitudinal psychiatric records are used to evaluate the proposed framework on standard summarization metrics and retrieval and temporal consistency proxy measures. The experimental results show that our model outperforms the fine-tuned model with ROUGE-1 score of 0.5034, ROUGE-2 score of 0.4026 and BERTScore F1 score of 0.8884. The framework also gains a temporal precision proxy score of 1.0000, representing perfect temporal consistency in the retrieval using clinician-annotated temporal correctness as the proxy. The findings suggest that the proposed framework could enhance the reliability of longitudinal psychiatric clinical summarization by providing well-founded facts, structural coherence, and temporal consistency.
