AB  - The paper will provide a survey of the recent developments in Sign Language Processing (SLP), the field in which there is a paradigm shift to a deep learning architecture based on accessibility equity rather than relying on traditional gloss-based pipelines. The survey critically examines the major advancements in Sign Language Recognition (SLR), Sign Language Translation (SLT), and Sign Language Production (SLP) and outlines the architecture of the evolution from the hybrid CNN-RNN models to Transformer-based frameworks and Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). It is observed that there is a definite trend of gloss-free translation, which is facilitated by discrete latent tokenization, vector quantization (SignLLM), and segment-aware encoding schemes like Low-SAGE. The survey also looks at the combination of heterogeneous sensory modalities, such as RGB and depth streams, skeletal grids, neuromorphic event cameras (VECSL), and electromyography (sEMG), to overcome issues such as motion blur and occlusion. Specific attention is given to linguistic inclusivity by examining large-scale standards of under-resourced sign languages, such as Indian (FDMSE-ISL), Persian, Bangla (BdSLW401), and Russian (Logos). This survey summarizes the ongoing challenges of the deployment of continuous recognition, retrieval-enhanced translation (OVSLT), and diffusion-based avatar synthesis (TDM, SOKE) and forms the outlook of how powerful, bi-directional, and human-friendly sign language foundation models can be built.
