AB  - Recent advances in Artificial Intelligence (AI), particularly Large Language Models (LLMs) and computationally intensive deep learning systems, have increased concerns about energy consumption and associated carbon emissions. This study presents a systematic literature review (SLR) of 55 primary studies examining the relationship between model-centric carbon efficiency and grid-responsive carbon awareness in Green AI. Following PRISMA 2020-guided study selection and narrative thematic synthesis, the review identified model-centric approaches, including pruning, quantization, knowledge distillation, and parameter-efficient methods, that reduce baseline computational and energy requirements. Carbon-aware approaches, including temporal load shifting, spatial workload migration, and telemetry-informed scheduling, adapt AI workload execution to variations in electricity-grid carbon intensity. The synthesis reveals that these research streams are often investigated independently, with persistent gaps in standardized carbon reporting, lifecycle assessment, and production-scale validation. The findings highlight the need for cross-layer approaches that integrate model-level efficiency with carbon-aware workload orchestration to support more environmentally sustainable AI systems.
ER  - 

