AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) offer transformative capabilities for automating literature reviews in specialized domains such as engineering and new energy research. We present an enhanced approach to fine-tuning a state-of-theart LLM with greater technical depth, emphasizing efficient adaptation techniques, robust data preparation, and optimized training on limited computing resources. Our methodology constructs a domain-specific dataset of literature in the new energy domain and formats it for supervised fine-tuning of a 7 billionparameter open LLM (e.g., LLaMA-7B), using cross-entropy loss minimization as the training objective. We discuss parameterefficient strategies (e.g., LoRA) that drastically reduce trainable parameters while maintaining performance [1], [2]. The model was trained on a multi-GPU setup with memory optimizations to handle the LLMs scale [3]. Quantitative evaluation using standard metrics for language generation-ROUGE for summarization quality and BLEU for content overlap-indicates improved output relevance after fine-tuning. The fine-tuned model demonstrates better recall of domain-specific terminology and produces more concise, informative summaries, reflected in higher ROUGE scores compared to the base model. We also analyze the trade-offs between model performance and computational cost: our fine-tuning approach balances accuracy gains against resource expenditure, ensuring the investment is justified by significant time savings in literature analysis. This work provides a blueprint for efficiently adapting LLMs to specialized research tasks, with implications for scaling AI solutions in scientific knowledge management across various engineering domains.
ER  - 

