AB  - Medical informatics is the field that combines healthcare, computer science, and information technology. Recent progress in Large Language Models (LLMs) has made it possible to make conversational systems that can answer medical questions. However, general-purpose LLMs frequently produce erroneous medical assertions, commonly referred to as hallucinations. When these systems are used to help with clinical work, these mistakes can be very dangerous. This study suggests a domain-specific generative medical chatbot that integrates Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA). The system gets peer-reviewed clinical evidence from the NCBI PubMed database and uses it to help create responses. The model lessens the chance of unproven medical claims by basing responses on verified literature. The Microsoft Phi-3-Mini model is used to build the framework. It has been fine-tuned with QLoRA and is connected to an FAISS vector retrieval pipeline. The system also supports multiple languages and can explain how it came up with its answers by using citation and confidence scoring. The experimental assessment has high levels of factual basis reaching F1 of 89.4% with a hallucination rate of only 2.1%. The proposed approach demonstrates that affordable hardware and local configuration of reliable medical conversation systems can be established and do not make things hidden and hard to repeat.
ER  - 

